      "text": "To determine which number is a multiple of every integer, we need to find a number \\( n \\) such that for any integer \\( k \\), \\( n \\) is divisible by \\( k \\). This means that \\( n \\) must be a multiple of every possible integer.\n\nLet's denote \\( n \\) as a multiple of \\( k \\). This implies that there exists some integer \\( m \\) such that \\( n = k \\cdot m \\).\n\nFor \\( n \\) to be a multiple of every integer, \\( n \\) must be a multiple of every positive integer. We need to identify the smallest such number.\n\n1. **Check if \\( n = 0 \\) is a multiple of every integer:**\n   - If \\( n = 0 \\), then for any integer \\( k \\), \\( 0 \\) is divisible by \\( k \\). This is true because \\( 0 = 0 \\cdot k \\).\n   - Therefore, \\( n = 0 \\) is a multiple of every integer.\n\n2. **Check if \\( n = 1 \\) is a multiple of every integer:**\n   - If \\( n = 1 \\), then there is only one integer \\( k \\) for which \\( 1 \\) is divisible by \\( k \\) (i.e., \\( k = 1 \\)).\n   - This is not true for all integers. For example, \\( 1 \\) is not divisible by \\( -1 \\).\n\n3. **Check if \\( n = -1 \\) is a multiple of every integer:**\n   - If \\( n = -1 \\), then there is only one integer \\( k \\) for which \\( -1 \\) is divisible by \\( k \\) (i.e., \\( k = -1 \\)).\n   - This is not true for all integers. For example, \\( -1 \\) is not divisible by \\( 2 \\).\n\n4. **Check if \\( n \\) can be any positive integer:**\n   - For any positive integer \\( n \\), \\( n \\) is divisible by every other positive integer as well.\n   - However, \\( n \\) must be divisible by all integers simultaneously, which is only possible if \\( n = 0 \\).\n\nSince \\( n = 0 \\) is the smallest number that can be a multiple of every integer, the number that is a multiple of every integer is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).",
